    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 199,
      "text": "MEXICO\nimproved cash generation has allowed for a rise in capital expenditures but will mostly be used for\ndebt amortization. PEMEX’s total debt outstanding has declined somewhat in recent years but\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 199,
      "end_char": 381,
      "text": "remains high, including to suppliers, and spreads on Pemex’s external debt continue to be above\nthose on bonds of the Mexican government and most oil sector peers.\nOUTLOOK AND RISKS\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 381,
      "end_char": 566,
      "text": "13. After the pickup in the first half, economic activity is expected to slow in the second\nhalf of 2022 and in 2023, given tighter global financial conditions and cooling U.S. growth.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 566,
      "end_char": 763,
      "text": "The outlook assumes a moderate further tightening in financial conditions, as Banxico and other\ncentral banks are expected to raise policy rates further, and fiscal policy stance is expected to be\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 763,
      "end_char": 954,
      "text": "neutral based on budget developments to date in 2022 and the proposed 2023 budget (Box 1).\nBased on past patterns, the recent tightening in financial conditions will modestly weaken domestic\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 954,
      "end_char": 1141,
      "text": "demand, consistent with the impact of higher costs of debt, lower free cash flow and retained\nearnings, and weaker investment and spending on durables. A more material impact is expected\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1141,
      "end_char": 1381,
      "text": "from the ongoing weakening of U.S. growth (Annex VII).\n14. Staff projects economic growth of 2.1 percent in 2022 and 1.2 percent in 2023. With\nthe projected weakening of both domestic and U.S. demand in the second half of 2022 and in 2023,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1381,
      "end_char": 1613,
      "text": "employment growth would also slow, reinforcing the deceleration in activity. The current account\nbalance is expected to slightly widen to about 1 percent of GDP in 2022–23, as import prices are\nincreasing faster than export prices.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1613,
      "end_char": 1847,
      "text": "Box 1. The 2023 Budget Proposal\nThe 2023 draft budget targets a small\nStaff Projection of 2022 and 2023 Budget (Share of GDP)\nincrease in the overall deficit due to the\n2022\nincrease in interest payments, 3.8 to 4.1\nStaff Report 2023\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1847,
      "end_char": 2028,
      "text": "percent of GDP. On this basis, staff Current\n2021\nestimates that the fiscal stance (based on GFS Revenue 23.2 24.3 24.1\nthe change in the structural primary Oil revenue 3.6 5.0 4.5\n"
    },
    {
